meta {
  name: Upload Screenshot
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

put {
  url: {{bugScreenshotUploadUrl}}
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: image/png
}

body:text {
  not-a-real-png-see-the-docs-block
}

tests {
  test("storage accepts the upload", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT <uploadUrl> — the raw screenshot upload (step 2 of 3)

  Not an API endpoint: this goes **straight to object storage** using the URL
  from `Presign Screenshot`. Same three-step chain as
  `Quote Recaps/Upload Quote Document` and `Deal Audits/Upload Audit Attachment`.

  - **Auth:** `none` — deliberately. The collection-level bearer would be sent
    on this request and **break the S3 signature**; a presigned URL carries its
    own credentials.

  ## Why this exists

  `Create Bug Report` calls `HeadObject` on every declared key and **drops** the
  ones nothing was uploaded to, so without a real PUT the screenshot would
  silently vanish from the report and the chain would prove nothing.

  ## Why the body is not a real PNG

  Storage holds opaque bytes and the API never decodes them — `statObject` reads
  only the size and the recorded content type. Nothing anywhere in the pipeline
  parses an image, so a text stand-in exercises exactly the same path. The
  four-line "PDF" in `Upload Quote Document` is the same trick for the same
  reason.

  ## Headers
  `Content-Type` **must** match the signed value (`requiredHeaders` from the
  presign response) or storage rejects the PUT with a 403 that says nothing
  useful.

  ## Response
  - `200 OK` — object stored; the key can now be attached to a report.
  - `403 Forbidden` — signature mismatch: usually an `Authorization` header
    being sent, a differing `Content-Type`, or an expired URL.
}
